  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe weather pattern is expected to shift late this week with a\ndeep trough getting carved out over the Pacific Northwest and\nsouthwestward to off the coast of California by Thursday. The\npolar jet associated with the northern stream system is expected\nto dip farther southward off the West Coast, while a stronger\nsubtropical jet across the central Pacific shifts eastward toward\nBaja. Although these jet branches are not expected to phase\ntogether going into Friday, there is likely to be some combined \nenergy reaching the Western U.S. \n\nAs the trough sets up to our west on Wednesday into Thursday, high\nlevel moisture will stream into our region, followed by increasing\nsoutherly moist flow in the lower levels Thursday night into\nFriday. Guidance is in very good agreement southerly low level \nmoist advection will combine with some top-down saturation in the \nupper levels on Friday across southern and central Arizona. PWATs \nare shown to quickly rise to just above 200% of normal by Friday \nmorning, while low level mixing ratios rise to a respectable 8-9 \ng/kg. This decent moisture is also expected to coincide with \nmodest low level upslope forcing and quite strong mid and upper \nlevel ascent within the left exit region of the subtropical jet. \nAlthough we are still four days out, confidence is quite good in \nshower activity blossoming at some point Thursday night/Friday \nmorning across south-central Arizona and lasting through much of \nFriday. Guidance also shows modest MUCAPE of 250-500 J/kg on \nFriday which should allow for some isolated to scattered weak \nthunderstorms. Shear values look to be on the low side, so at this\npoint we are not anticipating any strong storms. Forecast QPF \nfrom Thursday night through Friday night currently averages around\n0.50\" across the Phoenix area to upwards of 0.75-1.00\" in the \nhigher terrain north of Phoenix. The higher end of guidance is at \nleast double that, so it's not out of the question to have to \nworry about some minor flooding issues Friday. WPC has also \nincluded this area in a Slight Risk on their Day 5 ERO.\n\nThe Friday weather event will not be the last period of rain\nexpected over the next week as another likely even stronger system\nis forecast to track east northeastward toward southern California\nand northern Baja on Saturday before moving through our region\non Sunday. There is much more uncertainty with this second \nweather system compared to the first as there are notable timing \nand evolution differences. There are also some differences with \nthe potential phasing of the polar and subtropical jet streams \nwhich if it occurs would likely result in a decent low pressure \nsystem developing within the base of the large scale trough. \nModels at least agree that there will be as much if not better \nmoisture then the Friday system and stronger mid-level and frontal\nascent. Given the uncertainty, QPF amounts are still quite \nvolatile to any big changes in the strength of the system and \ntrack. However, so far ensemble mean QPF amounts indicate overall \nhigher average amounts compared to Friday's event, as well as more\nwidespread coverage. Rain chances could also extend into Monday, \nespecially across eastern portions of Arizona.\n\nTemperatures will take a hit later this week and through the\nweekend with NBM forecast highs dropping into a 75-80 degree range\nFriday and Saturday and even lower starting Sunday. The coolest \nair so far this fall should move in with the Sunday weather \nsystem as highs may fall to as the upper 60s to lower 70s for \nSunday and Monday.",
